# example.env for client/discord_bot.py — copy to .env (repo root) and fill
# in your values. The bot auto-loads .env via python-dotenv (pip install
# python-dotenv), and .env values override anything already exported in the
# shell.

# Required for client/discord_bot.py.
DISCORD_TOKEN=your-discord-bot-token-here

# ── Daily puzzle (Discord Activity) ─────────────────────────────────
# The /puzzle command wraps the activity in activity/. The bot owns none of
# the game; it calls three routes on the activity server: /api/today,
# /api/recap and, from /archive sync, /api/bot/reload-archive. See
# activity/README.md.
# Without PUZZLE_APP_ID, /puzzle still registers and explains what's missing.

# Discord application id of the activity — the same app the activity is
# published under. Used to build its launch link.
#PUZZLE_APP_ID=

# Base URL of the activity server, e.g. https://puzzle.example.com
#PUZZLE_API=

# Shared secret for the activity's bot-only routes. Must match BOT_API_KEY in
# activity/.env. Needed by the daily recap (/api/recap) and by /archive sync,
# to tell the activity to reload. Unset, the recap never posts and a sync
# publishes without the activity being told.
#PUZZLE_API_KEY=

# The daily recap: the day after each puzzle, a reply to that day's /puzzle
# announcement with how it went. It @-mentions every player it names, so
# everyone who played is pinged every day — which is why it is off unless this
# says on. Turning it on posts the previous day's recap as soon as the bot
# starts, then one a day.
#PUZZLE_RECAP=on

# Where the activity checkout lives, for `/archive sync`. That command runs
# `bun run sync-archive` as a subprocess, and Bun reads .env from the process
# working directory only — it does not walk up — so the sync has to start
# inside activity/ to see activity/.env at all. Leave it unset whenever the bot
# and the activity are deployed from one tree, which is the normal arrangement;
# the default is the activity/ beside this file.
#
# Who may run that command is not set anywhere: anyone may, at most once every
# 10 minutes across every server. A puzzle-admins.json left over from the old
# officer allowlist is no longer read.
#PUZZLE_ACTIVITY_DIR=

# ── Player bug reports (/report) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# `/report` files a GitHub issue on a player's behalf, so somebody can say
# "puzzle 46 is unsolvable" without making a GitHub account. Leave either of
# these unset and the command still registers, and tells whoever runs it,
# privately, that an officer has not finished setting it up. It does not say
# which key is missing; that is this file's job.
#
# Read this before you set them: the command publishes text typed by anybody in
# the server, under the bot's identity, to whatever repository you name — with
# the player's Discord display name in the title, which is what the club asked
# for. The bot defangs @mentions and #references so a report cannot become
# somebody else's notification, caps the description, and allows fifteen
# reports per player per hour, and sixty per server. None of that makes the
# repository private.
#
# A fine-grained token with **Issues: read and write on this one repository**
# and nothing else. Not a classic token, and not one that can push.
#GITHUB_TOKEN=
# owner/name of the repository issues are filed against.
#GITHUB_REPO=tetrisuci/BaronChairStair

# ── Running under the deploy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None of these is needed to run the bot by hand; they are for a box whose
# deploy gives each release its own directory and restarts the bot politely.
# docs/bot.md, "Under a deploy", says what each one does.
#
# This file OVERRIDES the process environment, for every setting below as for
# the token: a line here beats what the deploy (pm2, systemd) sets. So set each
# one in exactly one place — the deploy's environment or this file, never both.
# A STATUS_FILE here and another in pm2's environment and the bot writes a file
# the deploy never reads, and the deploy waits on "unknown"; a STATS_DB here
# and another there and the bot opens a database the deploy did not mean.

# The bot's database, as an absolute path. Unset, it is stats.db at the
# repository root, beside the code, as it always was. Set it once releases live
# in directories of their own, so they all share one file: the recap claims that
# stop a recap posting twice, the /archive sync window, the presence history and
# the last command sync live in it. A relative path is refused at start-up.
#STATS_DB=/home/you/bcs/shared/stats.db

# Where the bot writes its status for the deploy to read: counts only — how
# many commands are running, whether /archive sync is, when the last command
# was — rewritten every 5 seconds. Absolute. Unset, no file is written.
#STATUS_FILE=/home/you/bcs/shared/bot.status.json

# BUILD_ID, the release's commit, which the status file reports, is the one of
# these that never goes here: it changes with every release, and a BUILD_ID
# written here would beat the deploy's and name one build for all of them.
# The deploy sets it in the process environment. Unset, the status file says
# "dev".

# How long a stop (SIGTERM, or pm2's own SIGINT) waits for commands already
# running before the bot closes, in seconds; new commands are told to try again
# meanwhile. pm2's kill_timeout must be longer than this, or pm2 kills the bot
# part-way through the wait. And the stop must reach the bot alone — pm2's
# treekill: false, systemd's KillMode=mixed — or it reaches /highlights' replay
# bridge too, which dies at once. A second signal stops waiting.
#BOT_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_S=20

# The slash commands are written to Discord only when they have changed since
# the last write. Set this for one start to write them anyway — after somebody
# has changed them on Discord's side — then take it out again.
#FORCE_COMMAND_SYNC=1
